WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.547
After tokenization, the model receives numbers rather than words.

00:00:04.547 --> 00:00:08.292
Input IDs identify the vocabulary tokens.

00:00:08.292 --> 00:00:15.357
An attention mask indicates which positions contain meaningful input and which positions are only padding.

00:00:15.357 --> 00:00:20.267
Imagine a batch containing one short sentence and one longer sentence.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:25.442
The short sentence needs padding so both examples have the same tensor width.

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:31.227
Those padding positions should not influence the model in the same way as real text.

00:00:31.227 --> 00:00:35.387
The attention mask usually uses one for real positions and zero for padded positions,

00:00:35.387 --> 00:00:41.152
allowing the model to ignore the padding where appropriate.

00:00:41.152 --> 00:00:48.057
Do not confuse this padding mask with causal masking, which we will discuss later for decoder models.

00:00:48.057 --> 00:00:50.327
They solve different problems.

00:00:50.327 --> 00:00:56.237
When debugging Transformer input, printing both input IDs and the attention mask is one of the easiest ways

00:00:56.237 --> 00:01:01.178
to see whether the tokenizer created the batch you expected.
